==English==
[[File:Flodden Wall gunloops.jpg|'''embrasures''' in Flodden Tower, Edinburgh|thumb]]

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|frm|embrasure}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪmˈbɹeɪʒə(ɹ)/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|architecture|military}} Any of the [[indentation]]s between the [[merlon]]s of a [[battlement]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1938|author={{w|George Orwell}}|title={{w|Homage to Catalonia}}|chapter=6|url=http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks02/0201111h.html|passage=But there were less casualties than might have been expected, and the barricade rose steadily, a wall of concrete two feet thick, with '''embrasures''' for two machine-guns and a small field gun.}}
# The [[slanting]] indentation in a wall for a [[door]] or [[window]], such that the space is larger on the inside than the outside.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1916|author={{w|James Joyce}}|title={{w|A Portrait of the Artist as a Young Man}}|chapter=3|url=http://www.gutenberg.org/files/4217/4217-h/4217-h.htm|passage=When the fit had spent itself he walked weakly to the window and, lifting the sash, sat in a corner of the '''embrasure''' and leaned his elbow upon the sill.}}
#* '''2009''', {{w|Hilary Mantel}}, ''Wolf Hall'', Fourth Estate 2010, p. 155:
#*: Now he stands in a window '''embrasure''', Liz's prayer book in hand.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[embrace]].
#* '''1601''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''{{w|Troilus and Cressida}}'', Act IV, Scene 4, [http://www.opensourceshakespeare.org/views/plays/play_view.php?WorkID=troilus&Scope=entire&pleasewait=1&msg=pl]
#*: And suddenly; where injury of chance / Puts back leave-taking, justles roughly by / All time of pause, rudely beguiles our lips / Of all rejoindure, forcibly prevents / Our lock'd '''embrasures''', strangles our dear vows / Even in the birth of our own labouring breath:

